<Base title="About · Poshitha">
  <h1 class="page-title"></h1>

  <div class="story">
    <p>
      I grew up in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tirupati" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tirupati</a>,
      a small town in southern India known for being home to one of the
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venkateswara_Temple,_Tirumala" target="_blank" rel="noopener">largest temples</a>
      in the country.
    </p>
    <p>
      Growing up, I spent a lot of time doing math, competing in abacus
      tournaments, training in Bharatanatyam, drawing, and eating way too much
      ice cream. A pretty significant day in my life was when I got my first
      laptop in eighth grade and wrote my first "hello, world!" program. I've
      always loved both art and science. Over time, I've come to appreciate how
      thoughtful design paired with good engineering can make something not just
      functional, but intuitive and deeply human.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two people who shaped the way I see the world early on were my dad, whom I
      watched fall seven times and get up eight, and my mom, who has always
      brought people together through food, with a heart full of empathy and an
      incredible ability to put herself in someone else's shoes.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the final two years of high school, I moved across the country to
      attend <a href="https://www.jpischool.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jayshree Periwal International School</a>
      in Jaipur. Leaving home to live at a boarding school was one of the
      biggest turning points in my life. I met people whose lives looked
      completely different from mine, and the world suddenly felt much larger
      than the one I had known growing up.
    </p>
    <p>
      During high school, I started asking myself a question: what would I
      willingly stay up all night doing, even if nobody paid me? The answer I
      keep returning to is building technology alongside people who are
      excited to solve really hard problems.
    </p>
    <p>
      That journey eventually brought me to <a href="https://www2.lehigh.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lehigh</a>,
      where a full scholarship made it possible for me to study in the United
      States. Lehigh has become a second home to me, largely because of the
      people I have found here. I've spent my time developing skills in the
      subjects I care about, building <a href="/projects">projects</a> with some
      incredibly curious people, and bringing people together through
      <a href="https://www.lehighsof.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scholars of Finance</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout my life, I've been fortunate to be supported by scholarships
      and incredible <a href="https://www.dexterityglobal.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mentors</a>.
      That support has made me think a lot about access, what becomes possible
      with the right opportunity, and what I should do with the chances I've
      been given.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beneath everything I do is a fairly simple north star: I want my work to
      be useful to people beyond myself. I don't yet know what form that will
      take. I'm still learning what I should spend my time doing and what kind
      of work is uniquely mine to do. For now, I'm following my curiosity
      wherever it takes me, becoming more technically capable along the way, and
      trying not to take life or myself too seriously.
    </p>
  </div>

  <style>
    .story { max-width: 60ch; }
    .story p { margin: 0 0 18px; }
  </style>
</Base>
